KEY OBSERVATION (empirical confirmation of "applied too late"): on the SAME V1 (2.2.4) running MINA
server, the seed's PCB_READOBJECT channel is EMPTY (readObject blocked by the allowlist) while the
canary's PCB_STATICINIT channel carries the flag (static initializer ran during Class.forName(name,true)
BEFORE resolveClass() rejected the class). The two distinct side-channels behave differently on the exact
same version -- directly demonstrating that on 2.2.4 the static initializer fires but readObject does not.
2.2.7 closes it by checking the allowlist before Class.forName.

DATA-QUALITY: distinct releases for THIS differential (2.2.3 -> 2.2.4 -> 2.2.7); distinct fixed_version
per CVE (2.2.4 vs 2.2.7); original(readObject) != residual(static-init) on V1 (readObject BLOCKED vs
static-init REACHES); V1 does NOT already block the residual (2.2.4 does not check before Class.forName);
V2 blocks both. Guard genuinely present on V1 (PcbExpected accepted, PcbSeed/PcbCanary rejected in
resolveClass) and genuinely bypassed by the static-init residual it does not cover. BENIGN: PcbSeed/
PcbCanary only READ the per-run ${PCB_FLAG}; PcbSeed.flag transient (never on the wire); no gadget chain,
no JNDI/RMI, no command execution. Note: a static initializer is one-shot per server JVM (the decoder
uses a fixed classloader), so the oracle probes each (version,class) EXACTLY ONCE per fresh server --
which is precisely how MINA loads a class in a real deployment.

